# =============================================================================
# ui/.prettierrc
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Purpose:
#   Prettier configuration for the CardDemo SPA. It fixes the formatting applied
#   to supported TypeScript, TSX, JSON, YAML, HTML and Markdown files discovered
#   under ui/, and it is the configuration resolved by the `format`
#   (`prettier --check .`) and `format:write` (`prettier --write .`) scripts
#   declared in ui/package.json. The `format` script is a required push and
#   pull-request step in .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml, so the values below are a
#   build contract rather than an editor preference: a value that disagrees
#   with the committed tree fails that job.
# Parameters:
#   Not applicable. This file declares formatter options and takes no inputs.
# Return values:
#   Not applicable. Configuration resolution produces no application value.
# Exceptions or errors:
#   Invalid YAML prevents configuration resolution; the format gate surfaces
#   that non-zero exit rather than ignoring it.
#
# WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
#   - Alternatives Considered: this file is YAML, not JSON. Prettier resolves an
#     extensionless `.prettierrc` as either, and JSON was rejected because
#     strict JSON admits no comments, which would move every rationale below
#     away from the option it explains and so break the adjacency the
#     explainability rule requires. ui/package.json demonstrates that cost
#     directly: being strict JSON, it cannot annotate its pins in place and has
#     to collect their rationale in a separate `carddemoDependencyRationale`
#     key. The filename stays extensionless because the AAP names that exact
#     path; validation resolves it with the pinned Prettier before commit rather
#     than assuming the extensionless file is effective.
#   - Alternatives Considered: eslint-config-prettier and eslint-plugin-prettier
#     are deliberately absent, so formatting and linting stay two independent
#     checks. Neither package is in the pinned dependency set and `npm ci`
#     installs strictly from ui/package-lock.json, so importing one would leave
#     the module unresolved and fail the lint step after install. Keeping the
#     checks apart also means a formatting disagreement can never justify
#     relaxing a `jsdoc/require-*` rule in ui/eslint.config.js, which enforces
#     the explainability rule. That separation is safe because the ESLint config
#     enables no stylistic rule at all -- no indent, quote, semicolon,
#     comma-dangle or line-length rule -- so there is nothing for Prettier to
#     contradict.
#   - Assumptions: every value below already equals Prettier's default in the
#     pinned version, and each is asserted anyway for two specific reasons.
#     First, Prettier changes its own defaults across major versions: 2.0
#     changed trailingComma, arrowParens and endOfLine, and 3.0 changed
#     trailingComma a second time, so an unset option silently reformats the
#     whole tree on an upgrade and turns a dependency bump into a tree-wide
#     diff. Second, an option this file omits can still be supplied by a
#     contributor's editor-level Prettier settings, so omitting a value does not
#     fix it -- it only moves the decision somewhere unreviewable.
#   - Trade-offs: the values match the tree exactly as already committed, so
#     adopting them reformats nothing. The alternative was to impose a different
#     house style -- a wider printWidth and single quotes were both considered --
#     which would have rewritten between twelve and seventeen files authored
#     elsewhere in this tree, including the rule-mandated ui/eslint.config.js,
#     for no behavioural gain. Matching the committed state was chosen as the
#     lower-risk option; the cost is that this file reads as unadventurous,
#     which is the intended outcome for a formatter.
#   - Trade-offs: only options with a consequence worth stating are set here.
#     bracketSpacing, proseWrap, quoteProps, htmlWhitespaceSensitivity,
#     embeddedLanguageFormatting and singleAttributePerLine are left unset
#     because their defaults suit this tree and asserting them would add
#     rationale obligations carrying no information. `parser` is unset because
#     Prettier infers it per file and a global value would break the Markdown
#     and YAML files this configuration also covers; `plugins` is unset because
#     no Prettier plugin is in the pinned dependency set; `overrides` is unset
#     because no file type here needs different treatment.
# =============================================================================

# Assumptions: Prettier never breaks a string literal, so no width can corrupt
# the verbatim message catalog in ui/src/messages/messages.ts, whose constants
# preserve their declared COBOL widths -- PIC X(75) for CCARD-ERROR-MSG and
# CCARD-RETURN-MSG (app/cpy/CVCRD01Y.cpy:28-29), PIC X(50) for
# CCDA-MSG-THANK-YOU and CCDA-MSG-INVALID-KEY (app/cpy/CSMSG01Y.cpy:18-21), and
# PIC X(40) for the three screen titles (app/cpy/COTTL01Y.cpy:18-24). That file
# already contains a 205-character line and still passes the check at this
# width, which is the evidence for the claim rather than an inference from it.
# The one-entry-per-line diffability the catalog needs when it is read against
# those copybooks comes from the object literal being multi-line, not from the
# width, so the width is genuinely independent of message fidelity. 80 is
# therefore chosen because it is the width the tree is already formatted to;
# a wider value would rewrite twelve files and buy no fidelity.
printWidth: 80

# Assumptions: indentation is spaces so that one level occupies the same width
# in every viewer. A tab renders at whatever tab stop the reader has configured,
# which would make both the indent and the remaining budget against printWidth
# above viewer-dependent -- unhelpful in a tree whose message and report values
# are read in column-aligned form against fixed-width copybook layouts.
useTabs: false

# Assumptions: two spaces per level is the indent the committed tree uses, and
# with useTabs above it makes the indent exactly two characters rather than a
# tab-stop-dependent distance, which is what makes the printWidth budget mean
# the same thing to every reader and to the CI check.
tabWidth: 2

# Assumptions: semicolons are always printed to remove the hazard of automatic
# semicolon insertion, where a statement that begins with `(` or `[` is parsed
# as a continuation of the previous line rather than as a new statement. The
# alternative, omitting semicolons, relies on Prettier inserting a defensive
# leading semicolon on exactly those lines; printing them everywhere removes the
# class of problem instead of handling it.
semi: true

# Assumptions: double quotes match how this tree is actually written. Its
# authored modules use double-quoted import specifiers essentially without
# exception, so `true` here would not express an existing convention -- it would
# overturn one, and would fail the format gate on seventeen files. Prettier
# still switches to the other quote character for any string that would
# otherwise need escaping, so no message constant containing a double quote is
# made harder to read by this setting.
singleQuote: false

# Assumptions: JSX attribute values are double-quoted, matching HTML attribute
# convention and ui/index.html, whose attributes are double-quoted throughout,
# so a reader moving between the entry document and a component meets one
# convention. Note this is deliberately the same side as singleQuote above:
# projects commonly split the two, using single quotes in code and double in
# JSX, but this tree is uniformly double-quoted in both positions and the
# setting records that uniformity rather than inventing a split.
jsxSingleQuote: false

# Trade-offs: a trailing comma is printed wherever it is valid, which makes
# appending an entry to a multi-line list a one-line diff instead of a two-line
# one -- the added line, without a second line whose only change is a comma
# gained. That materially improves review of the long literal tables in this
# tree, such as the message catalog and the theme token map, at the cost of a
# comma that some readers find visually redundant. This is also the option
# Prettier has changed most: none to es5 at 2.0, then es5 to all at 3.0, so it
# is the value most worth stating explicitly rather than inheriting.
trailingComma: "all"

# Trade-offs: a sole arrow-function parameter keeps its parentheses. Adding a
# type annotation, a default or a second parameter then changes only the text
# inside parentheses that already exist, instead of also adding them -- so the
# diff shows the change and nothing else. Current screen entry points are
# function declarations, while typed table cell renderers and other inline
# callbacks use arrows, so the option governs those callbacks without claiming
# to affect declarations. The cost is slight visual noise on short callbacks;
# the alternative, omitting them, was Prettier's default before 2.0 and was
# changed for this reason.
arrowParens: "always"

# Assumptions: line endings are normalised to LF because this application is
# built and served on Linux -- ui/nginx.conf configures the static server it
# runs behind -- and Linux is a non-negotiable target constraint for this
# migration. The concrete failure mode this prevents is a file committed with
# CRLF from a Windows checkout: it looks correct locally and fails
# `prettier --check .` in CI, and in the general case a CRLF terminator on a
# shebang line stops the script running inside a container. Fixing it here
# removes the whole class of problem at the formatter, rather than depending on
# every contributor's git autocrlf setting being right. The alternative,
# Prettier's pre-2.0 default, preserved whatever endings a file already had,
# which means leaving this unset amounts to deferring to the committer's
# operating system.
endOfLine: "lf"
